Date: Wed, 17 Mar 93 00:45:32 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: reason

> One problem with this is, there is no rational, measurable,
> non-circular definition of "self-interest".

In my definition of "self-interest", there are several goals or requirements.
The more important of them tend to be prerequisites for those further down
the line, but my list would go:-
  
      Survival >> Sanity >> Physical Health >> Wealth >> Pleasure

If you can't even survive, you've lost every scrap of value you ever had,
and more importantly perhaps, the potential for living an exceedingly
enjoyable life in the future. To be insane is a rather dire predicament, but
it's not as bad as being 'information dead'. There would always be the
possibility of being cured.

However, being in physical ill-health would not be as bad as mental illness;
which might be uncurable even by cell repair machines. I certainly wouldn't
want to trade a few decades of probable life expectancy for wealth. But those
who manage to gain wealth should find it easier to attain the goal of 
pleasurable living in the short term, all else being equal. 

But it probably isn't. There is another factor, which could be termed
'ideological health' / 'spirit' / 'frame of mind' / 'memetic health' etc.
This would be more important than monetary wealth, for instance -- there are
plenty of cases of people having won fortunes, then frittering it away and
ending up worse than before. Memetic health should even protect against
insanity / disease / aging. Although these would hardly be conducive to
memetic health, so maybe it would be wrong to give it a weighting next to
'survival' on the scale. 

Survival, then, is what I would give priority to when assessing
'self-interest'. It's o.k. for memories and beliefs to change a bit at a time, 
but if a drastic change suddenly occured, unrelated to experience then this
would constitute death.

>> 3) It should optimise the subjective mental states and self-esteem of the
>> individual.

> This again provides no basis for measurement.  Can you demonstrate 
> that death does not constitute the optimal mental state?  To make 
> such a claim, you have to fall back on another set of beliefs, eg that
> death is bad.  Why is that belief valuable?  Etc.

Let's say I'm a deathist. I think death is the best thing since sliced bread,
and I'm always praising it. "There's nothing wrong with good ol' personal
annihilation. Tis good for you!"

Now I could claim that mental states do not require a material substrate --
'spirits' can fly in the air completely free of the material world. But the
scientific, rational approach should provide us with a more accurate
description of reality.

The schoolmen of the Middle Ages may have claimed that their beliefs were
based on reason, but they were hampered by a scarcity of empirical evidence.
Now that there isn't quite so much room for creative interpretations, our
current understanding is that all processes consist of one of four basic
interactions. If it is claimed that consciousness has mystical, ethereal 
characteristics then a 'fifth' force has to be postulated. There is no
evidence for this, and no evidence that identity can survive cremation.

Alternatively, I could take the altruist's approach: "Death is dignified,
respectable, necessary, and even desirable. It's great to know that, in being
sacrificed, you are contributing to the greater good of the whole."  

But how can this benefit anyone? It's not as if some end up winning while
others lose -- one generation is sacrificed and replaced, then the next
generation is sacrificed in its turn, etc. Until mortalism is finally realised
to be a gravely mistaken policy, but by then the altruists are extinct.

> The fundamental problem with these criterion are, how are they tied
> to our goal of anti-entropy?

I think extropy / entropy is only valuable insofar as it relates to
individuals. If all life in the universe was destroyed but the galaxies
managed to distribute themselves in a line so that the separation represented
pi in binary, we'd have failed in our goal. Life ought to achieve the goal of
extropy, with individuals surviving and going on to lead richly rewarding
lives. But I fear those who practise mysticism or altruism will reap only
entropy.

> Nick Szabo

