Date: Tue, 16 Mar 93 21:06:16 -0500
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: BRAIN: Information content

Derek Zahn writes (about the relatively low information content of 
the human genome...):

(BTW, the human brain is thought to contain about 10^11 neurons.  Thus, even
without considering the direct and indirect connections this structure is
several orders of magnitude greater than can be described by the genes)

>This implies: (1) Interaction with the environment is
>probably important in determining brain structure ("predestined
>learning"), (2) learning in general is overwhelmingly important,
>(3) there are severe limits to hardwired functionality, and 

Absolutely right! (IMHO)

Some people (myself currently included) believe that the brain develops as a
dynamical system driven mostly by environmental influences.  Note that from a
local viewpoint (say, some population of neurons), the "environment" includes
all the other such subsystems as well as other body tissue.  Thus it is the
epigenetic interaction of internal (genetic) biases and external
(environmental) properties that determines function (reminiscent of Dawkins'
extended phenotype theory).  Development of structures that are largely uniform
across individuals only appears genetically "hard-wired" but is really a
result of the very strictly controlled environment in the womb and the
ability to suppress some "noise" out.  After one exits into the world, this
development continues, but to what extend "learning" is driven by similar
mechanisms to "development" is unclear to say the least...

>(4) we have some hope of developing artificial specifications of
>similar complexity.

I agree (partly out of wishful thinking).  I'm currently interested in
modeling developmental processes such as specialization of cells in the visual
cortex, using competition and pruning of connections.  (In the brain,
competition appears all over the place.  Does this suggest that a competitive
society is, in general, an adaptive and stable system?)

>
>  * The natural substrate provided by the physics of organic
>    chemistry on the Earth's surface may not be optimal for
>    expressing a complex information processing system.
>

"Optimal" is a word rarely applicable to natural phenomena ("sufficient" is
usually more accurate).  Thus I believe you are right about this.  I hope that
once the principles by which the biological brain self-organizes are
understood, artificial adaptive organisms will be possible.  Then, perhaps we
will be able to analyze the functionality of these organisms and select out the
redundant dynamics and those with effects we decide are undesirable.  However,
such fundamental engineering may only be applicable to future human species, as
minds that have already established identity may lose it if their underlying
dynamics are radically changed.  (Of course, any such statements at this
immature stage of brain science are speculative...)

An exciting century awaits...

-Paul


