>Improved computers and communications can reduce the costs of these
>dynamic institutions, but the amount of human effort needed to
>establish the relationships needed seems to put a lower bound on the
>transaction cost of assembling an ad-hoc organization.

Are you are referring to the cost of figuring out, and writing, a  
contract---a specification of what you want done? 


If one knows at least part of one's business well enough to describe  
it in a contract, then, for that part of one's business, one obtains  
market efficiencies (and organizational agility).  If one knows no  
part of one's business well enough to describe it in a contract, then  
(ignoring the tax code, which distorts organizational practices) one  
can always contract for persons to behave like employees: "Do what I  
tell you when I tell you to do it, because I'm an ignorant manager  
who doesn't know what I want done and/or I am unable to delegate."   
Thus, you can efficiently simulate a conventional bureaucracy with an  
ad-hoc org.  That is, the bureaucracy is a degenerate case, where the  
manager is an ignoramus and the contracts are essentially employment  
contracts.  Seen this way, I believe that ad-hoc orgs need never be  
less efficient than existing orgs.  (I say "need never" because one  
might write a bad contract.)

-Pete
