Date: Tue, 16 Mar 93 21:10:36 EST
From: clayb@cellar.org
Subject: META: Why not moderation?

[ NOTE: I'm not sure how this happened, but at the end of one of Tim
May's posts on an unrelated subject, I found most of what appeared
to be a post about moderation of the list. I'm assuming Tim did post
that material intentionally, and that the BBS software where I am
somehow garbled it to the end of another of his messages. ]

Tim May writes:

>> 1. It smacks of central planning, of censorship, of the antithesis
   of much of what we believe in.
      
Tim, I respect you a great deal, but this is just specious reasoning.
(or should I say "sloppy" <g>). First, as I understand it, Extropians
are not against "centralization", per se. Should there be an ExI, a
president, board, etc? Of course. It's useful order; and no one is 
forcing it on anyone. Choosing what goes on this list is defending 
our property: our time.

In addition, Saying that you cannot publish everything everywhere is not 
censorship, at least not in any meaningful sense of the word. The 
alternative is bedlam: _The Economist_ becomes _Mother Jones_. 

>> 2. Moderation, even by someone who reads nearly every posting, would
   inevitably introduce time delays into the process.	

Which would you rather have: time-delays, or noise? Besides, with
someone who is well-connected and reads the list often, the marginal
time-delay shouldn't be onerous.
  
>> 3. Who would moderate such an argumentative bunch?	

Weeding out the foo-foo posts and fellow travellers should not be a very
demanding task. Otherwise, I imagine we would be just as free to argue
away on important points, if not freer, due to the loss of friction.
																											 
>> But I agree with Clay that we are beginning to drown.
   Are there more ideas out there?

Sure. Make two lists: extropians-m and extropians, where the former is
moderated traffic, and the latter gets everything. Let's see which one
the market chooses.

One final point: moderation does not have to be the solution for all time.
If a better idea comes along, then moderation could be dropped. But for
the meantime, moderation is an elegant solution to the problem of too
much noise on the list.

Cheers

Clay


